  "notes": [
    "Reflexive compound of *cruzar*, with the same spelling change: **z→c before e**, seen in the yo form of the pretérito indefinido (me entrecrucé) and throughout the subjuntivo presente and imperative forms (entrecruce, entrecrucemos, entrecrucen).",
    "Past participle is regular: **entrecruzado**.",
    "Typically used with plural or collective subjects, since crossing/intertwining requires more than one thing: *sus vidas se entrecruzan* (\"their lives become intertwined\").",
    "Imperativo afirmativo reflexive pronouns attach to the end with a written accent to preserve stress (entrecrúzate, entrecrúcese, entrecrucémonos, entrecrúcense); imperativo negativo places them separately in front (no te entrecruces...).",
    "*Sus vidas y sus ilusiones se entrecruzan en los escasos paradores.* (\"Their lives and hopes become intertwined at the scarce roadside stops.\")",
    "*Los caminos se entrecruzaron sin que lo supieran.* (\"Their paths crossed without them knowing.\")",
    "Related: *cruzar* (non-reflexive base verb, \"to cross\"), *cruzarse* (reflexive, \"to cross paths\"), *entrelazarse* (synonym, \"to become interwoven\")."
  ],
